# Line-ending policy — makes checkouts deterministic across macOS/Linux/Windows.
#
# Git for Windows ships with core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites LF -> CRLF on
# checkout. That breaks two things the `org` engine relies on:
#   * .patch files — `git apply` matches byte-for-byte and fails on CRLF.
#   * .sh scripts  — bash chokes on the `\r` in a CRLF shebang / `set -euo pipefail`.
#
# `.gitattributes` overrides each user's core.autocrlf per-file and travels with the
# repo, so every clone (no per-machine setup) gets the same line endings.

# Safety net: git auto-detects text and stores it LF in the repo, so nobody can ever
# commit CRLF. (Working-tree eol stays platform-native unless pinned below.)
* text=auto

# These MUST be LF in the working tree too, on every platform.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.patch text eol=lf
